# Bow-Fault Math

**Ingredients:** MONGOLIAN MORIN KHUUR + MATH ROCK / POST-HARDCORE POLYMETER + PREPARED VIBRAPHONE + BARITONE SAXOPHONE + FLOOR-TOM KIT + ANALOG TAPE

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a cold-season rehearsal residency inside a retired geological survey workshop outside Ulaanbaatar, where morin khuur player Saruul Erdene was testing long bow-pressure crescendos against drafting tables covered with seismic cross-sections while guitarist-producer Adrian Cole was writing interlocking 7/8 and 11/8 figures for a small math-rock ensemble. The useful connection appeared when Saruul stopped treating odd meter as something to count and instead mapped bow pressure to the shape of each cycle. Soft bow contact marked the beginning of a phrase, stronger pressure created the internal stress point, and a deliberate rasp or harmonic fracture marked the place where the meter turned over. Prepared vibraphone, baritone saxophone, and a floor-tom-centered drum kit were added to build a muscular chamber-rock form in which rhythmic structure behaves like material under load.

Bow-Fault Math is an acoustic-forward progressive rock form built around the relationship between bowed friction and irregular meter. The morin khuur is the principal melodic and textural instrument, not a decorative folk layer. Its two strings, horsehair bow, vocal-like glides, open fifths, harmonics, and pressure-dependent rasp are used to articulate meter physically rather than simply melodically.

The style avoids the polished tapping-guitar stereotype often associated with math rock. Guitars may appear, but the primary interlock is distributed among morin khuur, baritone saxophone, vibraphone, toms, and bass. The ensemble favors short cells whose lengths differ but reconnect at clear structural points: a seven-eighth bowed figure against an eleven-eighth vibraphone ostinato, a five-beat saxophone phrase crossing a four-beat tom cycle, or a three-note bass figure that resolves only after several bars.

The morin khuur is recorded with one close ribbon microphone aimed toward the bridge-body area, a second condenser positioned higher to capture bow noise and harmonics, and a dry room microphone placed several meters away. The close ribbon preserves wood, body resonance, and low mids. The upper microphone captures horsehair friction, finger slides, harmonic whistle, and pressure changes. The room microphone remains modest so the ensemble retains physical scale without becoming cinematic.

Players favor open fifth drones, narrow pentatonic cells, fourths, minor sevenths, minor-second pressure tones, overtone glissandi, double-stop pulses, stopped-string growls, and sharply released bow attacks. Long lyrical melodies are possible but are usually interrupted by meter turns, bow-pressure changes, or ensemble cutoffs. The morin khuur should sound like a bowed structural beam carrying rhythmic stress.

The central compositional device is the **Stress Cycle**. Every irregular phrase is divided into three physical states: LOAD, HOLD, and FRACTURE. LOAD introduces the cell with controlled bow contact and moderate ensemble density. HOLD sustains or repeats the pattern while one or more layers remain metrically offset. FRACTURE is the structural release point: a rasped morin khuur attack, tom choke, baritone-saxophone burst, vibraphone mute, or full-band cutoff marks where the pattern either reconnects or mutates. The device is not tied to a specific time signature. A 7/8 phrase may use 3+2+2, while an 11/8 phrase may use 4+3+4 or 3+3+2+3; the stress point determines how the grouping is felt.

Prepared vibraphone provides the fixed metallic lattice. Selected bars are damped with felt, rubber, paper, binder clips placed safely on resonant hardware, or hand pressure. Motor vibrato is usually disabled. The instrument plays dry dyads, octave anchors, fourth stacks, asymmetrical repeated notes, and occasional bowed-vibraphone tones. Its function is to expose the exact pulse grid against which the morin khuur can bend and rasp.

Baritone saxophone supplies a reed-based low-mid counterforce. It plays short unison stabs with the morin khuur, breath-heavy pedal tones, slap-tongue punctuation, split-note attacks, low fifths, and abrupt chromatic falls. It does not behave like a jazz soloist. Its role is structural: thickening selected stress points and providing a blunt air-column contrast to string friction.

The rhythm section centers on floor toms rather than cymbal wash. Kick drum is compact, snare is dry, and cymbals are sparse. Large toms carry the shape of irregular groupings through low, resonant strokes, muted rim hits, hand-damped heads, and brief rolling pickups. The drummer must make odd meter feel inevitable rather than intellectual. When the ensemble enters 11/8, the listener should feel a repeated physical contour before recognizing the count.

Electric bass is usually clean or lightly overdriven and often plays fewer notes than the other instruments. Long pedals, displaced roots, octave jumps, and one- or two-note cells stabilize sections that would otherwise become harmonically overactive. In heavier passages, bass distortion is allowed only if the note attack remains clear enough to reveal meter.

Guitar is optional and secondary. When used, it favors dry single-note counterlines, muted harmonics, open-string dyads, pick scrapes, prepared-string buzz, and short chord clusters. Continuous high-gain walls are excluded from the core style. Distortion belongs at fracture points, not across the entire arrangement.

Analog tape is treated as a textural document of stress rather than a nostalgia effect. Selected transitions may be printed hot enough to create saturation, slight compression, and momentary edge softening. Tape stops, reverse effects, and obvious lo-fi warble are rare. The preferred sound is a mostly clean recording whose loudest pressure events bend the medium slightly.

The defining tension is control versus material failure. Every phrase is carefully counted, but its emotional force comes from sounding as if the physical act of bowing, striking, blowing, and damping might exceed the structure holding it together.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 96–132 BPM, with frequent 5/8, 7/8, 9/8, 11/8, mixed-meter 4/4-to-7/8 transitions, and polymetric overlays. Groupings must be physically legible through accent shape rather than presented as abstract counting exercises.

**Harmonic Palette:** Open fifths, Mongolian pentatonic centers, Dorian and Aeolian modes, quartal stacks, minor 7ths, suspended 2nds, octave pedals, minor-second stress tones, and occasional tritone pressure at fracture points. Harmony is typically static or slow-moving so rhythm and bow color remain dominant.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Stress Cycle** — irregular phrases move through LOAD, HOLD, and FRACTURE states, with timbre and density changing according to structural pressure rather than conventional verse/chorus logic.
2. **Horsehair Accent Map** — morin khuur bow pressure marks internal meter groupings: soft contact for pickup, full tone for structural body, rasp or overtone break for stress points.
3. **Tom Geometry** — floor toms and muted drum heads carry the main odd-meter contour while cymbals remain sparse.
4. **Metal Grid** — prepared vibraphone establishes fixed, dry pulse coordinates against the flexible pitch and friction of bowed strings.
5. **Reed Brace** — baritone saxophone reinforces only selected fracture points, low pedals, and unison attacks instead of providing continuous harmonic filling.
6. **No-Complexity Fog** — every additional meter, layer, or preparation must remain audible as a distinct function; dense playing that obscures the cycle is removed.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through stronger bow pressure, closer metric overlap, denser tom groupings, more aggressive baritone-saxophone articulation, harder vibraphone mallets, and shorter silence between fracture points. Climaxes may become heavy, but they should remain dry, articulate, and ensemble-driven rather than collapsing into generalized noise.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Bow-Fault Math formed inside a retired geological mapping and seismic survey workshop on the outskirts of Ulaanbaatar. The building retained long drafting tables, topographic drawers, rolled fault maps, mineral cabinets, steel rulers, hanging work lamps, acoustic ceiling panels, survey tripods, cracked linoleum, and shelves of old core samples labeled by depth.

Morin khuur player **Saruul Erdene** had been using one of the drafting rooms to document pressure-based bow articulations for an experimental notation project. Rather than cataloguing pitches alone, she marked how the sound changed as bow weight increased: clean onset, saturated fundamental, overtone flare, rasp, and eventual collapse.

American-British guitarist and composer **Adrian Cole** was in Mongolia through a small interdisciplinary residency focused on rhythm and spatial notation. He had been writing polymetric guitar music but was dissatisfied with how easily complex meter became a technical display. Hearing Saruul’s pressure tests suggested a different solution: meter could be made physical by attaching its internal accents to changes in friction.

Their first successful piece used one seven-eighth phrase repeated without harmonic change. Saruul increased bow pressure on the final two eighth notes of every cycle while Adrian removed the guitar entirely and asked the drummer to mirror only the pressure contour on floor toms. The rhythm immediately became easier to feel while sounding more severe.

Prepared-vibraphone player **Nomin Batbayar** joined after creating short metallic ostinati whose deadened resonance made odd subdivisions unmistakable. Baritone saxophonist **Elias Ward** added low reed braces at selected stress points. Drummer **Temuulen Ganzorig** rebuilt his kit around two floor toms, one compact kick, dry snare, and minimal cymbals. Bassist **Mara Voss** completed the group by providing long pedal tones and deliberately sparse displaced roots.

Their first performances were called **fault readings**. Large paper cross-sections were placed behind the musicians, but instead of geological labels they showed the grouping of each composition as blocks of unequal length. A thick black line marked the FRACTURE point. The diagrams did not control electronics; they served as visual scores so both musicians and audience could see where pressure accumulated and released.

The scene developed a visual language of graphite grey, mineral white, rust red, dark horsehair brown, survey orange, matte black, faded blue drafting paper, and cold fluorescent work light. Clothing remained practical: heavy wool, work jackets, dark trousers, boots, no folklore costume and no prog-rock theatricality. The unofficial rule is that if a meter cannot be felt by someone who is not counting, the arrangement has failed.

