# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Seven Eighths Across the Fault Marker

**Story & Context:** The debut opens at 108 BPM with Nomin playing a prepared-vibraphone cell in 7/8 grouped 3+2+2. The first three eighth notes ring slightly longer, while the final four are heavily damped. Saruul enters with one open-fifth morin khuur double stop and increases bow pressure across the same grouping: clean on the first three, denser on the middle pair, and rasped across the final two.

Temuulen does not mark every subdivision. He plays one floor-tom stroke at the beginning of each grouping, making the seven-beat structure feel like three blocks rather than a stream of counts. Mara sustains one low pedal for four complete cycles before changing pitch. Elias enters only on the FRACTURE point with one short baritone-saxophone burst.

At the midpoint, Adrian introduces a guitar phrase in 5/8 against the continuing 7/8 cycle. The two patterns reconnect after thirty-five eighth notes, but the arrangement does not emphasize the mathematical coincidence with a dramatic hit. Instead, Saruul abruptly reduces bow pressure to nearly nothing, making the reunion feel like structural relief.

The final section removes guitar and saxophone. Vibraphone, floor toms, bass pedal, and morin khuur repeat the original phrase three times. On the fourth cycle, Saruul refuses the expected rasp and lets the final note remain clean, ending the track without fracture.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close overhead photograph of a geological fault map with seven hand-drawn rhythmic blocks crossing a thick black fault marker, a horsehair bow laid diagonally over the paper, prepared vibraphone mallet and steel survey ruler nearby. No conventional lyrics; sparse spoken counting fragments limited to “three, two, two,” “hold the line,” “pressure on the last pair,” and “do not break this time.”

## Track 2: Core Sample 41 Splits at Eleven

**Story & Context:** The second track runs at 124 BPM and uses an 11/8 cycle grouped 4+3+4. Temuulen establishes the contour with low floor tom, muted snare, and a second floor tom, leaving cymbals completely absent. Mara plays a two-note bass figure that lasts only seven eighth notes, causing its root placement to drift across the larger cycle.

Saruul performs short stopped-string morin khuur figures rather than drones. Her first phrase remains clean, the second includes overtone whistle, and the third pushes into deliberate bow rasp on the eleventh eighth note. Nomin answers the rasp with one hard muted vibraphone octave, while Elias plays a low baritone-saxophone fifth that begins after the metric boundary rather than on it.

Adrian’s guitar enters in the second half with dry harmonics repeating every five eighth notes. Because none of the secondary layers share the 11/8 length, the arrangement produces a rotating field of alignments around the stable tom contour.

For the final sixteen cycles, elements are removed rather than added. Guitar disappears first, then saxophone, then bass. The last remaining pattern is morin khuur against the two floor toms. Saruul increases bow pressure on each repetition until the final eleventh-note fracture becomes nearly noise, then stops instantly.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cylindrical geological core sample labeled 41 resting in a wooden tray, a visible split line aligned with the eleventh mark on a hand-drawn metric ruler, morin khuur bridge and baritone-saxophone bell entering the frame from opposite sides. Spoken fragments limited to “sample forty-one,” “four, three, four,” “split at eleven,” and “leave the crack open.”

## Track 3: The Map Reconnects Where Nobody Counts

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 98 BPM in apparently straightforward 4/4. Saruul plays a slow pentatonic morin khuur phrase, Mara holds long bass roots, and the drums use only kick and one floor tom. After sixteen bars, Nomin introduces a vibraphone ostinato in 9/8 while the rest of the ensemble remains in 4/4. The shift is intentionally subtle.

Elias adds a five-beat breath-heavy baritone phrase. Adrian plays a seven-eighth muted guitar figure so quietly that it functions more like a recurring mechanical texture than a riff. Over several minutes, the listener loses any single obvious downbeat while each individual musician remains perfectly consistent.

The ensemble reconnects only when all secondary cycles happen to arrive near the same structural point. Instead of accenting that convergence, every player drops out except Saruul. She continues one open fifth alone for a full bar, then the band re-enters in unison 6/8 with the simplest groove on the EP.

The final passage contains no polymeter. Morin khuur, bass, vibraphone, baritone saxophone, and toms repeat one six-beat figure while gradually reducing dynamics. The conceptual resolution is that complexity was never the destination; the purpose of the irregular cycles was to create enough pressure that plain shared pulse could feel newly physical.

